WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.410 --> 00:00:02.533
<v Examiner>Health Science 2.

2
00:00:02.533 --> 00:00:02.995
<v Examiner>Test 3.

3
00:00:02.995 --> 00:00:04.845
<v Examiner>Every short-answer question.

4
00:00:05.295 --> 00:00:05.574
Hey.

5
00:00:05.574 --> 00:00:10.260
This is the Test 3 episode of the SAQ podcast for Health Science 2.

6
00:00:10.260 --> 00:00:14.316
It has one job: every written question Test 3 can ask you,

7
00:00:14.316 --> 00:00:19.491
answered the way she marks them, and the one-mark facts her quiz keys ask.

8
00:00:19.491 --> 00:00:20.889
One topic per track.

9
00:00:21.339 --> 00:00:23.026
Where the questions come from.

10
00:00:23.026 --> 00:00:25.387
She doesn't post a list of test questions.

11
00:00:25.387 --> 00:00:27.636
In her own words, there is no such list.

12
00:00:27.636 --> 00:00:31.233
The closest thing, she says, is the module's learning checklist,

13
00:00:31.233 --> 00:00:33.201
because it limits what she may ask.

14
00:00:33.201 --> 00:00:36.461
So every point on her Module 3 checklist has a track here.

15
00:00:36.461 --> 00:00:38.204
It starts with her own signals.

16
00:00:38.204 --> 00:00:43.095
The two case studies she names for Test 3: Case 11, Pania's milk coming in, and Case L,

17
00:00:43.095 --> 00:00:44.219
Jack's hearing loss.

18
00:00:44.219 --> 00:00:48.717
Pedigree charts, because in her own words, printed on six of her quiz questions,

19
00:00:48.717 --> 00:00:51.021
pedigree charts will be tested in Test 3.

20
00:00:51.021 --> 00:00:54.226
And the topics on her help board: glaucoma and cataracts,

21
00:00:54.226 --> 00:00:57.543
conduction versus sensorineural deafness, and Huntington's.

22
00:00:57.543 --> 00:01:01.084
After that comes every question on her student-marked SAQ quiz,

23
00:01:01.084 --> 00:01:02.771
and the rest of her checklist.

24
00:01:02.771 --> 00:01:07.212
The answers are hers wherever she has posted one: her model answers, her decks,

25
00:01:07.212 --> 00:01:07.999
her quiz keys.

26
00:01:07.999 --> 00:01:12.440
Where her feedback and her own slides disagree, you'll hear which one to write,

27
00:01:12.440 --> 00:01:15.419
and where an answer isn't hers, you'll hear that too.

28
00:01:15.869 --> 00:01:16.609
Quick facts.

29
00:01:16.609 --> 00:01:19.998
Test 3 is thirty-seven questions in sixty-five minutes,

30
00:01:19.998 --> 00:01:23.141
and about thirty per cent of the marks are written.

31
00:01:23.141 --> 00:01:26.098
That's roughly fifteen marks you write yourself.

32
00:01:26.098 --> 00:01:29.056
It closes on Monday the twenty-sixth of October.

33
00:01:29.056 --> 00:01:31.336
Check your own booked time on Canvas.

34
00:01:31.786 --> 00:01:37.400
Her question verbs for this test, from her Assessment Overview: explain, analyse, apply,

35
00:01:37.400 --> 00:01:38.421
and demonstrate.

36
00:01:38.871 --> 00:01:43.281
How she marks a written answer: about half a mark for each correct fact.

37
00:01:43.281 --> 00:01:46.590
So a four-mark question wants about eight short facts.

38
00:01:46.590 --> 00:01:47.570
Not a paragraph.

39
00:01:47.570 --> 00:01:51.184
A list, one fact per line, with the mark word in each line.

40
00:01:51.184 --> 00:01:54.615
Every answer in this episode comes to you in that shape.

41
00:01:55.065 --> 00:01:56.334
How every track works.

42
00:01:56.334 --> 00:02:00.835
First you'll hear the question in a second voice, the way it might be printed.

43
00:02:00.835 --> 00:02:03.836
Then the hook: one line to hang the whole answer on.

44
00:02:03.836 --> 00:02:07.240
Then the answer, part by part, in the order you'd write it.

45
00:02:07.240 --> 00:02:09.952
Then the traps, where marks usually go missing.

46
00:02:09.952 --> 00:02:12.953
And last, the whole answer once more, in one breath.

47
00:02:12.953 --> 00:02:17.742
The final track, Rapid-fire, is every one of those one-breath answers back to back.

48
00:02:18.192 --> 00:02:19.026
How to use it.

49
00:02:19.026 --> 00:02:21.470
The first time through, just let it play.

50
00:02:21.470 --> 00:02:22.483
Don't take notes.

51
00:02:22.483 --> 00:02:27.191
The second time, when the examiner asks for it once more at the end of a track,

52
00:02:27.191 --> 00:02:29.217
say your answer before I say mine.

53
00:02:29.217 --> 00:02:31.720
And when one feels shaky, loop that track.

54
00:02:31.720 --> 00:02:36.368
The player page can loop one track, or play each one twice before it moves on.

55
00:02:36.818 --> 00:02:41.311
The order: the two cases first, then pedigrees, Huntington's, and glaucoma,

56
00:02:41.311 --> 00:02:43.348
because those are her own signals.

57
00:02:43.348 --> 00:02:48.679
Then the rest of genetics, the senses, and reproduction, each ending with its fine print:

58
00:02:48.679 --> 00:02:53.412
the one-mark facts, in her quiz keys' own words, that no written answer covers.

59
00:02:53.412 --> 00:02:57.186
And just before Rapid-fire, one more track of closed questions.

60
00:02:57.186 --> 00:02:57.725
Let's go.
